Dear Changi Chapel and Museum team,

I write Lesser Known Singapore (https://pikaia.github.io), a small blog
about
overlooked parts of Singapore's history, with narrated video versions of
each
post on YouTube (https://www.youtube.com/@LesserKnownSingapore) and TikTok.

I have just published a post on the Changi Murals and Stanley Warren:
https://pikaia.github.io/2026/10/24/the-changi-murals-stanley-warrens-chapel-paintings-at-roberts-barracks/

The post describes the murals in words only, because I have not been able to
find any freely licensed photograph of them. It points readers to the
museum as
the place to see the replicas. I would like to add one or two pictures of
the
murals, and I would be grateful for your advice on three points:

1. Could the museum supply, or allow me to use, a photograph of the replica
   murals on display? If it is easier, could I visit and photograph them
myself
   for this use?

2. Does the museum or the National Heritage Board hold photographs of the
   original murals in Block 151 that could be licensed, or can you tell me
who
   does?

3. Do you know who holds the copyright in Stanley Warren's paintings today
(his
   family, the Ministry of Defence, or another party), so that I can ask
them
   too if needed?

The pictures would appear in the blog post (no advertising) and in its
narrated
video on YouTube and TikTok, which may show platform advertising. Each
picture
would carry the credit line you specify, with a link to the museum. Web
resolution is enough.

If there is a fee or a form I should use instead, please let me know.

Thank you for your help, and for keeping the story of the murals in front of
visitors.

Yours sincerely,
Chris Lee
Lesser Known Singapore
https://pikaia.github.io

